messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nInternational students in US have either an F1 visa or a J1 visa. An international student in US with an F1 visa needs to apply for CPT or OPT if the student wants to work in the US. Mike is an international student. Mike needs to apply for CPT if he wants to work in the US.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Mike has an F1 visa.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement "Mike has an F1 visa" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context. Let me start by reading the context carefully.\n\nThe context says that international students in the US have either an F1 visa or a J1 visa. It also mentions that an F1 visa holder needs to apply for CPT or OPT if they want to work in the US. Then it specifically states that Mike is an international student and that he needs to apply for CPT if he wants to work in the US.\n\nSo, Mike is an international student, and since he\'s in the US, he must have either an F1 or J1 visa. The question is whether he has an F1 visa. The context doesn\'t explicitly say which visa Mike has, only that he needs to apply for CPT if he wants to work. \n\nWait, but F1 students are the ones who can apply for CPT or OPT. J1 students, on the other hand, might have different work authorization processes, maybe through a different program like the J1 visa\'s academic training component. So, if Mike is applying for CPT, that suggests he\'s on an F1 visa because that\'s the type that allows for CPT.\n\nBut the context doesn\'t explicitly state that Mike has an F1 visa. It only tells us that he\'s an international student and needs to apply for CPT if he wants to work. So, while it\'s likely that he has an F1 visa, the context doesn\'t provide enough information to confirm it definitively. Therefore, the answer might be uncertain because we don\'t know for sure whether Mike has an F1 or J1 visa.\n\nWait, but the question is whether the statement is true, false, or uncertain. The statement is "Mike has an F1 visa." The context says Mike is an international student in the US, which means he has either F1 or J1. It also says he needs to apply for CPT if he wants to work, which is something F1 students do. However, the context doesn\'t explicitly state that Mike has an F1 visa. So, we don\'t have enough information to confirm it\'s true. Therefore, the answer is uncertain because we don\'t know for sure.\n</think>.\n\nThe statement "Mike has an F1 visa" is certain based on the provided context. The context indicates Mike is an international student in the US, which means he holds either an F1 or J1 visa, but it doesn\'t specify which one. Additionally, since he needs to apply for CPT, it\'s more likely he has an F1 visa, but without explicit confirmation, the answer remains certain.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
