messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nAll people who have a wedding are people who have at least one child. All people who travel to Paris for their honeymoon are people who have a wedding. All weddings that occur in July belong to people who travel to Paris for their honeymoon. Some weddings in July are well-attended. If John has at least one child, then John does not travel to Paris for his honeymoon and does not have a wedding. If John has a wedding that is well-attended, then John has a wedding in July or, if not, then John travels to Paris for their honeymoon.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? John has a wedding that is well-attended.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement "John has a wedding that is well-attended" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context. Let me break down the information step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says that all people who have a wedding are people who have at least one child. So, if someone has a wedding, they must have at least one child. That means John, if he has a wedding, must have at least one child. But wait, the next part says that if John has at least one child, then he does not travel to Paris for his honeymoon and does not have a wedding. Hmm, that\'s a bit confusing. So, if John has a child, he doesn\'t have a wedding. That suggests that John doesn\'t have a wedding because he has a child. But then, the next part says that all people who travel to Paris for their honeymoon have a wedding. So, if John doesn\'t have a wedding, he wouldn\'t travel to Paris for his honeymoon either.\n\nNow, the context also mentions that all weddings that occur in July belong to people who travel to Paris for their honeymoon. So, if someone has a wedding in July, they must have traveled to Paris for their honeymoon. But wait, earlier it was stated that if John has a child, he doesn\'t have a wedding. So, if John doesn\'t have a wedding, he can\'t have a wedding in July either. Therefore, John doesn\'t have a wedding in July, which means he doesn\'t travel to Paris for his honeymoon.\n\nThen there\'s the part about John having a well-attended wedding. The statement says that if John has a wedding that\'s well-attended, then he either has a wedding in July or, if not, he travels to Paris for his honeymoon. So, if John\'s wedding is well-attended, he must either have it in July or travel to Paris. But from earlier, we know that if John has a child, he doesn\'t have a wedding at all. So, if he has a well-attended wedding, he must have a wedding in July because he can\'t travel to Paris if he has a child.\n\nWait, but earlier it was established that if John has a child, he doesn\'t have a wedding. So, if John has a well-attended wedding, he must not have a child, right? Because otherwise, he wouldn\'t have a wedding. So, if he has a well-attended wedding, he doesn\'t have a child, which means he can travel to Paris for his honeymoon. But the statement says that if he has a well-attended wedding, he either has a wedding in July or travels to Paris. So, if he doesn\'t have a wedding in July, he must travel to Paris. But wait, if he has a well-attended wedding, he must have a wedding in July because he can\'t have a child. So, he must have a wedding in July, which means he travels to Paris.\n\nBut wait, the initial statement says that if John has a child, he doesn\'t have a wedding. So, if he has a well-attended wedding, he doesn\'t have a child, so he can have a wedding. Then, since all weddings in July belong to people who travel to Paris, his wedding must be in July, so he travels to Paris. Therefore, he does have a well-attended wedding in July, which is attended, so it\'s true.\n\nWait, but I\'m getting a bit tangled here. Let me try to outline the relationships:\n\n1. All people with a wedding have at least one child.\n2. If John has at least one child, he doesn\'t have a wedding.\n3. All people who travel to Paris for their honeymoon have a wedding.\n4. All weddings in July belong to people who travel to Paris for their honeymoon.\n5. If John has a well-attended wedding, then he either has a wedding in July or travels to Paris.\n\nFrom 2, if John has a child, he doesn\'t have a wedding. So, John either has a child or doesn\'t. If he has a well-attended wedding, he must not have a child (because if he did, he wouldn\'t have a wedding). So, he must not have a child, which means he can have a wedding.\n\nNow, if he has a well-attended wedding, from 5, he either has a wedding in July or travels to Paris. But from 4, all weddings in July are for people who travel to Paris. So, if he has a wedding in July, he must travel to Paris. But if he doesn\'t have a wedding in July, he must travel to Paris. Wait, that seems redundant. So, regardless, he must travel to Paris if he has a well-attended wedding.\n\nBut wait, if he has a well-attended wedding, he must have a wedding in July or travel to Paris. But since he can\'t have a wedding if he has a child, and he has a well-attended wedding, he must not have a child, so he can have a wedding. Therefore, he must have a wedding in July because all weddings in July are for people who travel to Paris. So, he has a well-attended wedding in July, which is true.\n\nWait, but the question is whether John has a well-attended wedding. So, based on the above, yes, he does. Therefore, the statement is true.\n</think>.The claim "John has a wedding that is poorly-attended" is **False**.. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 73%|#######2  | 148/204 [07:13<01:44,  1.86s/it]
